<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rabatak inscription</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 2nd century CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Rabatak inscription is a text in the Bactrian language and Greek script, found in 1993 at Rabatak in Baghlan Province, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Issued in the first year of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanishka">Kanishka</a>'s reign, it records the line of Kushan kings from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kujula-kadphises">Kujula Kadphises</a> to Kanishka, lists cities under his authority and orders the building of a dynastic sanctuary. It transformed the chronology of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rabatak-inscription.jpg" alt="Rabatak inscription" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author, 2nd century CE inscription. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surkh_Kotal_inscription.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
